ese changes didn't just impact  bondholders directly they also affected  investors like Pension funds who held  derivatives such as interest rate swaps  these are Financial contracts whose  values are tied to assets like bonds  they were doing a lot with borrowed  money it's called Leverage and in the  financial world and that means that you  can buy Hedges or protect against  interest rate swings or inflation very  cheap but that means also whenever  interest rates move like they did in  recent days really unprecedented moves  you're exposed many times over to hedge  potential losses derivatives contracts  often also require collateral from  investors usually this is held in the  form of cash or Securities like bonds  but cash is preferred contracts often  also require additional collateral to be  posted if those trades begin to lose  money for example if interest rates move  that was the case for some investors who  held these derivatives when bond yields  started to rise and because changes were  happening so quickly collateral was  requested on a tighter timeline than  usual normally you have maybe a week to  get that money to your counterparty in  this situation they were asking for it  within hours Pension funds that didn't  have enough cash on hand to put up for  collateral sold off some of their bonds  this created a feedback loop selling  more bonds drove down prices even more  which in turn resulted in more calls for  collateral which led to more bonds being  sold off  well I think what this showed is is the  risks of derivatives and trading with  borrowed money AKA leverage we've seen  this before in the financial crisis but  this was once again a reminder of how  quickly the market can turn around on  you and the widespread impacts I can  have on financial markets justifying its  intervention the bank of England said on  Wednesday were to function in this  market to continue or worsen there would  be a material risk to UK Financial  stability the bank of England will  continue to buy bonds through October  14th but effects from the crisis have  spilled into other markets so it's not a  long-term strategy there's a lot of  questions about what's happened what's  going to happen once they stop buying  those bonds I think this is serving as a  reminder of the risks that lie ahead as  central banks continue with their plans  to raise interest rates to combat  inflation  foreign
 

